WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:10.170
Well, Democrats may have an intention of resisting
Donald Trump on a lot of issues in his first

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.120
100 days and in the first two years of his
administration.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.110
They don't have the votes to effectively stop
him, at least not in the House of Representatives

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:21.430
where they're at one of the low points they've
had since 1928.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.630
They have enough votes in the Senate to sustain
a filibuster, but as the outgoing Democratic

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:32.800
leader, Harry Reid, pointed out, he changed
the filibuster rules for lower federal court

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.930
nominations, and there's nothing to stop the
Republican majority from changing them for

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:41.590
Supreme Court nominations, which one will
be on tap, or on other issues.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.250
I think an interesting point is whether or
not the Republicans can move ahead on immigration

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:51.610
and can peel off some Democratic votes here.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.630
And also on infrastructure issues.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:58.100
The Democrats say they want to build more
infrastructures, so does the president-elect.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.700
Will some of them go along with a package
that he will put in that might get away with

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:03.700
certain obstructions?

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:04.469
We'll see.

